    "reply": "BRCA1 is linked to four diseases: familial cancer of breast [1], familial breast-ovarian cancer susceptibility 1 [2], pancreatic cancer susceptibility 4 [3], and Fanconi anemia complementation group S [4]. The gene symbol BRCA1 [5] and gene name BRCA1 [9] are identified in records alongside the OMIM entry BRCA1 DNA REPAIR-ASSOCIATED PROTEIN; BRCA1 [8], consistent with a tumor suppressor whose mutations account for approximately 40% of inherited breast cancers and more than 80% of inherited breast and ovarian cancers [15: \"approximately 40% of inherited breast cancers and more than 80% of inherited breast and ovarian cancers\"].\n\n## Gene Function and DNA Repair\n\nBRCA1 encodes a 190 kD nuclear phosphoprotein that maintains genomic stability and acts as a tumor suppressor [15: \"plays a role in maintaining genomic stability, and it also acts as a tumor suppressor\"]. It participates in DNA repair [18], regulation of DNA repair [23], double-strand break repair via homologous recombination [12], and DNA damage tolerance [28]. Publications describe the BRCA1-BARD1 complex promoting RAD51-mediated homologous DNA pairing [6] and its mechanism in DNA end resection and DNA protection [24].\n\n## Variants and Clinical Evidence\n\nClinVar records include NM_007294.4(BRCA1):c.5243_5277+2788del [7], NM_007294.4(BRCA1):c.5277+2916_5277+2946delinsGG [14], GRCh37/hg19 17q21.31(chr17:41267743-41267796)x3 [25], and NM_007294.4(BRCA1):c.2145C>G (p.Thr715=) [30]. Preneoplastic stromal cells have been shown to promote BRCA1-mediated breast tumorigenesis [19], and targeting GPX4-mediated ferroptosis protection sensitizes BRCA1-deficient cancer cells to PARP inhibitors [29]. BRCA1 is also described as an endocrine and metabolic regulator [13].\n\n## Clinical Trials and Publications\n\nClinical trials include a study of germline BRCA1 and BRCA2 mutations in Jewish women affected by breast cancer [10], an investigation of BRCA1 haploinsufficiency and gene expression [16], a study customizing treatment according to RAP80 and BRCA1 in advanced lung carcinoma [21], and a letrozole prevention trial in postmenopausal women with a BRCA1 or BRCA2 mutation [26]. Additional publications are identified by PMID 28976962 [11], PMID 35432218 [17], PMID 36914836 [22], and PMID 39261728 [27]. A large multi-subunit protein complex known as the BRCA1-associated genome surveillance complex forms through combination with other tumor suppressors, DNA damage sensors, and signal transducers [15: \"large multi-subunit protein complex known as the BRCA1-associated genome surveillance complex (BASC)\"].",
